        "### 4. Crime Type Dynamics — How has the composition of crime types (violent vs. property vs. other) shifted between 2020 and 2022, and which categories show the most volatility?\n",
        "\n"
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "3aBK7YhvtRmB"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Findings: <br>\n",
        "\"Property\" type crimes are the main composition of the crimes being committed for each individual year.\n",
        "<br>\n",
        "Categories such as \"Prostitution\", \"Public Peace Violation\" and \"Interference with Public Officer\" were the most volatile, with \"Prostitution\" being the most volatile with a 0.557 volatility index."
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "id": "dcOxWQzKmNOi"
      }
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "import plotly.express as px\n",
        "\n",
        "# --- Visualization 1: Shift in Broad Categories (2020-2022) ---\n",
        "# Using the permanently added 'Crime_Category' column\n",
        "category_by_year = df.groupby(['Year', 'Crime_Category']).size().reset_index(name='Count')\n",
        "\n",
        "fig_composition = px.bar(\n",
        "    category_by_year,\n",
        "    x='Year',\n",
        "    y='Count',\n",
        "    color='Crime_Category',\n",
        "    title='Shift in Crime Composition (2020-2022)',\n",
        "    barmode='group',\n",
        "    labels={'Count': 'Number of Incidents', 'Crime_Category': 'Crime Category'},\n",
        "    color_discrete_map={'Violent': '#e74c3c', 'Property': '#3498db', 'Other': '#95a5a6'}\n",
        ")\n",
        "fig_composition.update_layout(xaxis=dict(tickmode='linear'))\n",
        "fig_composition.show()\n",
        "\n",
        "# --- Visualization 2: Identify Most Volatile Individual Crime Types ---\n",
        "# Calculate yearly counts for individual crime types\n",
        "volatility_df = df.groupby(['Year', 'Primary Type']).size().unstack(fill_value=0).T\n",
        "\n",
        "# Measure volatility using Coefficient of Variation (Standard Deviation / Mean)\n",
        "# Focus on crimes with an average of at least 100 incidents per year to filter out low-frequency noise\n",
        "volatility_df = volatility_df[volatility_df.mean(axis=1) >= 100]\n",
        "volatility_df['Std_Dev'] = volatility_df.std(axis=1)\n",
        "volatility_df['Mean'] = volatility_df.mean(axis=1)\n",
        "volatility_df['Volatility_Index'] = (volatility_df['Std_Dev'] / volatility_df['Mean']).round(3)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Sort and get top 10 most volatile crimes\n",
        "volatile_crimes = volatility_df.sort_values(by='Volatility_Index', ascending=False).head(10).reset_index()\n",
        "\n",
        "fig_volatility = px.bar(\n",
        "    volatile_crimes,\n",
        "    x='Volatility_Index',\n",
        "    y='Primary Type',\n",
        "    orientation='h',\n",
        "    title='Top 10 Most Volatile Crime Categories (Min 100 Yearly Avg Incidents)',\n",
        "    labels={'Volatility_Index': 'Volatility Index (Std Dev / Mean)', 'Primary Type': 'Crime Type'},\n",
        "    color='Volatility_Index',\n",
        "    color_continuous_scale='OrRd'\n",
        ")\n",
        "fig_volatility.update_layout(yaxis={'categoryorder':'total ascending'})\n",
        "fig_volatility.show()"
